---
// StackScore, a closer look (public, ungated), with Call-and-Response folded in as a related
// experiment (the plan Andy approved Nov 8). SuppCo work is kept generic (Andy, Nov 8): shipped UI and
// the one public-safe result line (Master Profile §2.2). Sources: story kit §4.5, §2.2 (StackScore and
// Call-and-Response rows), and the Figma 2026 Portfolio › Case Studies › "StackScore case study" and
// "Call-and-Response case study" slides, whose own copy this follows. Left out: the "What it moved"
// slide (all are call-only, and the update result is rounded to "about a quarter" in public: refresh rate, email click rate, retention, Pro
// likelihood, return rate), the share card naming a colleague (cropped from the original-score
// figure), and every Call-and-Response test number (call-only). Its result direction, “activation, not
// monetization,” is public by Andy’s call (Nov 9). No dates.
import CloserLook from '../../../components/site/CloserLook.astro';
import site from '../../../content/site.json';
import { SITE_BASE, CALL_PATH } from '../../../lib/site-sections';
import { tokens } from '../../../lib/tokens';
const accent = (tokens.accent as Record<string, string>).suppco;
const work = site.work.find((w) => w.id === 'stackscore')!;
const img = (name: string) => `/site/work/stackscore/${name}.webp`;
---

<CloserLook
  title={work.title}
  subtitle="SuppCo’s signature score, redesigned to explain itself"
  company={work.company}
  pageTitle="StackScore · SuppCo · Andy Detskas"
  description="How I redesigned SuppCo’s signature score into a hub that explains itself, with four sub-scores that each lead to a next step."
  accent={accent}
  backHref={`${SITE_BASE}work/`}
  backLabel="Selected work"
>
  <p class="panel-lede">I redesigned SuppCo’s signature score into a hub that explains itself, breaking one AI-written verdict into four sub-scores that each lead to a next step.</p>
  <dl class="look-facts">
    <div>
      <dt>My role</dt>
      <dd>Designer, from the first framing through launch and a year of iteration</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Worked with</dt>
      <dd>Product and engineering, who owned the scoring logic</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>What I designed</dt>
      <dd>The four sub-scores, the ring, the Insights and Optimize pattern on every tab, and the update flow</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <p class="outcome">After launch, an update prompt raised score updates by about a quarter for free and Pro members.</p>
  <figure class="look-fig">
    <img src={img('hub-tabs')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="The StackScore hub’s Overview, Value, Goals and Dosage tabs: a ring score of 91 with four segments, and each tab’s own score and insights" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
  </figure>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="situation">
    <h2 id="situation">The situation</h2>
    <div class="look-split">
      <div class="look-text">
        <p>The score was why people downloaded SuppCo, yet it was a single number and a long, AI-written report about your supplement stack.</p>
        <p>The score moved for reasons no one could see, and when members followed our advice, they didn’t always understand why it changed. It came up again and again in member feedback.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="look-fig">
        <img src={img('original-score')} width="949" height="1013" alt="The original score: one number on an orange or green card, followed by a long written analysis" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="role">
    <h2 id="role">My role</h2>
    <div class="look-text">
      <p>I designed StackScore from the first framing through launch and a year of iteration. Product and engineering owned the scoring logic; I owned how the score looked, read and behaved, and what it asked members to do next.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="approach">
    <h2 id="approach">How I approached it</h2>
    <ol class="look-steps" role="list">
      <li>
        <p class="look-num" aria-hidden="true">01</p>
        <h3>Four questions, not one verdict</h3>
        <p>I broke “How’s my stack?” into the four questions people actually ask: Am I buying quality? Spending well? Covering my goals? Taking the right amount? It also showed the score for what it was: a calculation that moves with your stack, not something ambiguously AI-generated.</p>
        <figure class="look-fig">
          <img src={img('four-questions')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="“How’s my stack?” broken into four sub-scores: Quality, Value, Goals and Dosage, each with its own question" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="look-num" aria-hidden="true">02</p>
        <h3>Find the ring</h3>
        <p>Four sub-scores couldn’t bury the one number people came for. I tried bars, petals, stacked arcs and color fields, with Apple Watch faces as inspiration. One ring with four segments won: the score stays the hero, and each segment shows at a glance where you’re strong and what to fix.</p>
        <figure class="look-fig">
          <img src={img('ring-explorations')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="Ring explorations, from a single gauge to segmented arcs, ending in the shipped ring with four segments" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="look-num" aria-hidden="true">03</p>
        <h3>Every score earns an action</h3>
        <p>A score without a next step is a dead end. Each tab pairs Insights (why you got this score) with Optimize (what to do about it): swap a low-quality product, find better value, fill a goal gap, fix a dose. Optimize also became the natural home for Pro tools.</p>
        <figure class="look-fig">
          <img src={img('insights-optimize')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="The Quality tab with Insights and Optimize, a Pro product optimizer, and a drawer for fixing a goal gap" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="look-num" aria-hidden="true">04</p>
        <h3>Make the update a moment</h3>
        <p>Scores went stale because updating was invisible. I modeled the update on a credit report: a short calculation, a from-to reveal, and a log of what changed. Monthly emails brought people back for a new score, even when they hadn’t opened the app.</p>
        <figure class="look-fig">
          <img src={img('update-moment')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="The update flow: an update drawer, a calculating screen, the score moving from 79 to 88, and the refreshed hub" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="changed">
    <h2 id="changed">What changed</h2>
    <div class="look-text">
      <p>After launch, the new update prompt raised score updates by about a quarter for both free and Pro members, and StackScore became one of SuppCo’s most established engagement loops.</p>
      <p>It didn’t fix everything. New members still needed a faster path to their first score.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="related">
    <h2 id="related">A related experiment: call and response</h2>
    <div class="look-text">
      <p>SuppCo’s onboarding asked new members about their goals, age, budget and stack without saying what they’d get back. My concept was to treat every question as a call that earns a response: answer, and SuppCo answers back with a small, specific fact about you or people like you, proof of value before the paywall instead of a pitch.</p>
      <p>Onboarding was about to change again, so I built it to test, not to keep. One template (an icon, a headline, two proof points) turned every new response into copy and data rather than new screens, and we shipped it as an A/B test. It moved activation, not monetization: more new members committed their stack, which the rest of SuppCo builds on, but it didn’t sell Pro on its own.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="look-fig">
      <img src={img('call-and-response')} width="1561" height="1139" alt="Call and response: the question “Where do you need the most help?” with “Track my supplements” selected, answered by a screen on staying consistent with your routine" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="look-section" aria-labelledby="learned">
    <h2 id="learned">What I learned</h2>
    <ul class="look-takeaways" role="list">
      <li>Watch for anything that slows the habit you’re trying to build.</li>
      <li>A score needs reasons and a next step.</li>
      <li>Build experiments to be replaced.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <aside class="look-cta" aria-label="Talk it through">
    <p>The full story, with what the tests showed and what I’d change, is a 30-minute walkthrough.</p>
    <a class="btn" href={CALL_PATH} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      {site.intro.cta.label}
      <span class="sr-only">(opens in a new tab)</span>
    </a>
  </aside>

  <p class="look-back"><a href={`${SITE_BASE}work/`}>← All selected work</a></p>
</CloserLook>
